      "credit": "https://www.flickr.com/photos/59081381@N03/54369502705/",
      "description": "Founded in 1864, the Mayo Clinic was founded by Dr. William Worrall Mayo as a small independent medical practice upon his move to Rochester, Minnesota, and grew over the next half-century into a significant regional hospital.  The clinic grew in response to the efforts and prestige by Dr. Mayo and his sons, whom followed in his footsteps as medical doctors, and in the 1890s, various other physicians joined the practice, growing it from a small operation into a hospital.  The hospital continued to grow, and built a six-story building on the present campus in the early 20th Century, which was later supplemented by the construction of the Plummer Building and Kahler Grand Hotel before World War II, both of which housed additional amenities and treatment centers for patients.  After World War II, the clinic continued to grow, building a massive new building across the street from the Plummer Building in 1955, and building subsequent structures to expand the campus during the subsequent decades.  In 1972, the clinic opened the Mayo Medical School, and opened campuses in other cities in the late 20th Century and early 21st Century  Today, the Mayo Clinic is one of the leading research hospitals in the United States, and employs 7,300 physicians and 66,000 support and administrative staff, and is the primary employer in the city of Rochester, Minnesota.",
